	return {"title":"Your laboratory equipment guide","topic":0,"difficulty":0,"source":"Lab / Volt equipment reference","questions":[],"theory":"","procedure":"","data":"","seed":0,"guide_pages":[
	{"title":"AC bench · meters and loads","text":"Find the tall equipment racks along the AC wall. All three racks have the same layout.\n\nTOP LEFT — AC AMMETER\nMeasures current: how much electricity flows through a path. Put it IN the path, using the ± socket and one numbered current-range socket under the SAME meter. Start with a range larger than the current you expect. Do not connect it directly between the supply and N.\n\nTOP MIDDLE — THREE-PHASE WATTMETER\nMeasures real power in watts. Its current connection goes in the circuit path; its voltage connection measures between two points. Use the wiring diagram for your particular assignment. Keep negative readings negative.\n\nTOP RIGHT — AC VOLTMETER\nMeasures voltage BETWEEN two points. Use the black ± socket and a yellow range socket under the SAME meter. The sockets on this panel are labelled 100 and 250. Use 250 when unsure.\n\nSECOND ROW — RESISTANCE / INDUCTANCE / CAPACITANCE\nLeft: resistors use electrical power. Middle: inductors store energy in a magnetic field. Right: capacitors store energy in an electric field. Each panel has three separate groups. A group's top and bottom sockets connect that group to your circuit. Each of its three switches adds the value printed below that switch. Several ON switches connect those values in parallel."},
	{"title":"AC bench · supply and machines","text":"BOTTOM LEFT — POWER SUPPLY\nProvides electricity for the circuit. I means ON; O means OFF. The big dial adjusts the VARIABLE outputs. The first socket column, 1/2/3/N, is fixed AC. The second, 4/5/6/N, is variable AC. Red 4 is at the TOP of the second column. Its N return is at the BOTTOM of that column. The small selector chooses the displayed voltage. DC 7/N is variable up to 120 V, DC 8/N is fixed at 120 V, and LOW 0/1 is 24 V AC.\n\nTHIRD ROW LEFT — DATA ACQUISITION\nAnother way to read measurements. E1–E4 measure voltage between each pair of sockets. I1–I4 measure current when inserted into a path. Click Computer I/O to see the AI and DI readings and set AO and DO outputs. AI and DO use the DAQ signal common. Each DI uses its paired return.\n\nTHIRD ROW MIDDLE — IGBT CHOPPER / INVERTER\nPosts 1 and 2 are the DC bus. Posts 3 through 5 are the three outputs. Apply a 0–5 V signal between a gate and IGBT COMMON. Gates 1/2, 3/4, and 5/6 select the upper or lower switch for each output. DISABLE high opens all outputs. An external pulse generator can drive a gate in a coupled circuit.\n\nTHIRD ROW RIGHT — TRANSFORMER\nChanges AC voltage using coupled windings. Top sockets 1–2 are the 120 V winding. Other winding sections are marked with their own voltage ratios. Their numbers identify sockets, not the voltage currently present.\n\nBOTTOM MIDDLE — ELECTRODYNAMOMETER\nApply 120 V AC across DYN 0 and DYN 1 to excite the brake. The load knob then changes how hard it brakes the motor. Hold the measuring control while taking the modeled readings.\n\nBOTTOM RIGHT — INDUCTION MOTOR\nConverts three-phase electricity into rotation. The three winding pairs are 1–4, 2–5 and 3–6. Follow the assignment's wye or delta connection diagram."},
	{"title":"DC bench · power and components","text":"Find the three DC benches on the opposite wall. Use the oscilloscope screen to open the instrument controls. Each bench has its own circuit.\n\nTRIPLE DC POWER SUPPLY\nThree separate adjustable outputs: CH1, CH2 and CH3. Each has + and − sockets. Set voltage and current limit, enable the channel, then enable master output. CV means the supply is holding voltage. CC means it has lowered voltage to keep current at the limit. Outputs are isolated until you wire them together.\n\nGENERATOR\nCreates a changing signal. Connect GEN and REF to the two circuit points you want to drive. Select sine, square, triangle, sawtooth, pulse or DC. Frequency is how often it repeats. Amplitude is its peak size; offset shifts it above or below zero. The output resistance affects the voltage delivered to a load.\n\nBUILT-IN PARTS\nR1a/R1b connect resistor 1; R2a/R2b connect resistor 2. Ca/Cb connect the capacitor. Da/Db connect the diode. Change values in the Leads & components tab.\n\nBREADBOARD CONNECTIONS\nConnect B1–B4 to a deployed breadboard from Leads & components. The breadboard editor lets you choose which hole each B socket reaches. A–E holes on one numbered row are connected; F–J on that row are a separate strip. P and N rails split halfway down the board."},
	{"title":"DC bench · measuring and getting started","text":"DIGITAL MULTIMETER\nFor voltage, connect VΩ and COM ACROSS the two points to compare. For current, put A and COM IN the circuit path. The A socket contains a low-resistance shunt and a fuse, even when another mode is selected.\n\nResistance, continuity and diode modes inject a small test current. Disable circuit power sources before using those modes. OL means the meter cannot obtain a reading in that test range. A blown current fuse must be replaced with outputs disabled.\n\nOSCILLOSCOPE\nShows voltage changing with time. Connect S1 or S2 to the signal, and GND to its reference. Both channels share GND. Timebase changes the time shown across the screen; volts/div changes vertical scale. The trigger starts a capture when the chosen signal crosses a level. Single captures once. The traces come from the connected circuit, not a preset picture.\n\nCABLES\nBoth labs use colored leads from the drawer beneath the bench. Open the drawer, choose a lead, then click the two destination sockets in order. Right-click a plug to detach that end. Press Backspace to return the held lead.\n\nREADING HIGH PANELS\nAim at an AC socket or control and press F for a straight-on inspection view. Use the mouse to aim and the wheel to zoom. Press F or Escape to return.\n\nSTART SMALL\nBegin with one supply and one resistor. Measure voltage, then current. Check I = V/R. Add one new part only after you understand the first reading."}
